WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.080
Good morning Hank, it's Tuesday.

00:00:01.080 --> 00:00:04.490
So Bill and Melinda Gates's annual letter
just came out, and it really is my favorite

00:00:04.490 --> 00:00:08.710
letter of the year...with apologies to the
birthday card I get every year from my insurance

00:00:08.710 --> 00:00:09.710
agent.

00:00:09.710 --> 00:00:10.710
You should read the whole thing, link below.

00:00:10.710 --> 00:00:14.059
But it predicts that in the next 15 years,
the lives of the world's poorest people will

00:00:14.059 --> 00:00:15.650
become dramatically better.

00:00:15.650 --> 00:00:16.650
All right.

00:00:16.650 --> 00:00:19.930
So even though hundreds of millions of people
have emerged from absolute poverty in the

00:00:19.930 --> 00:00:23.859
last 25 years, and even though child mortality
has been cut by half, and even though more

00:00:23.859 --> 00:00:28.939
kids than ever have access to vaccinations,
we still kind of live in two worlds.

00:00:28.939 --> 00:00:34.300
In one world, fewer than 1% of kids die before
the age of 5, almost no one gets malaria,

00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:39.540
very few children are denied access to vaccines
because of lack of refrigeration, and serious

00:00:39.540 --> 00:00:42.250
malnutrition in children is extremely uncommon.

00:00:42.250 --> 00:00:44.240
And then, we have the other world.

00:00:44.240 --> 00:00:49.010
In that one, 1.5 million kids die every year
from diseases that could be easily prevented

00:00:49.010 --> 00:00:50.010
by vaccines.

00:00:50.010 --> 00:00:54.730
There are like 200 million cases of malaria
every year, and more than 25% of kids are

00:00:54.730 --> 00:00:58.830
stunted due to malnutrition, which can lead
to cognitive problems and all kinds of other

00:00:58.830 --> 00:00:59.830
health issues.

00:00:59.830 --> 00:01:02.960
And in this world, kids are far less likely
to go to school, or to learn how to read,

00:01:02.960 --> 00:01:04.690
especially if they're girls.

00:01:04.690 --> 00:01:08.110
And all of that is totally unacceptable.

00:01:08.110 --> 00:01:09.820
It's also unnecessary.

00:01:09.820 --> 00:01:14.000
So global child mortality has dropped by half
in the last 25 years, and the Gates letter

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:18.000
argues that in the next 15 years, it can drop
by half again.

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:21.800
And this is achievable without expensive specialists
or new medical procedures.

00:01:21.800 --> 00:01:22.800
How?

00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:23.800
First, breast feeding.

00:01:23.800 --> 00:01:27.660
Infants who are breastfed for 6 months are
14 times more likely to survive.

00:01:27.660 --> 00:01:31.960
Second, access to cheap injectable antibiotics,
which could save 300,000 kids a year.

00:01:31.960 --> 00:01:36.190
Then you've got inexpensive hand-pumped oxygen
masks, which could prevent a third of infant

00:01:36.190 --> 00:01:37.190
deaths.

00:01:37.190 --> 00:01:40.390
Fourth, drying the baby and offering skin-to-skin
contact to keep it warm.

00:01:40.390 --> 00:01:42.780
And lastly, keeping the umbilical cord free
from infection.

00:01:42.780 --> 00:01:46.390
This can actually be done with an ingredient
found in my mouthwash.

00:01:46.390 --> 00:01:47.420
Literally.

00:01:47.420 --> 00:01:50.640
Before moving on here, quick reminder that
cutting infant mortality does not lead to

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:52.810
population explosions in the long run.

00:01:52.810 --> 00:01:54.270
In fact, quite the opposite.

00:01:54.270 --> 00:01:56.810
The Gates letter also talks about a bunch
of other investments and innovations that

00:01:56.810 --> 00:02:01.010
will improve the lives of poor people, like
access to mobile banking and better farming

00:02:01.010 --> 00:02:02.820
practices and online education tools.

00:02:02.820 --> 00:02:05.830
And I just wanna imagine for a moment what
that world would look like.

00:02:05.830 --> 00:02:08.610
Enough food would be produced in Africa to
feed every African.

00:02:08.610 --> 00:02:13.220
The 2.5 billion people without a bank account
would be able to save and dramatically simplify

00:02:13.220 --> 00:02:15.530
their economic lives with access to mobile
banking.

00:02:15.530 --> 00:02:20.980
Girls would have far better educational opportunities,
dramatically reducing the gender gap in literacy.

00:02:20.980 --> 00:02:25.760
Polio, river blindness, and guinea worm diseases
that kill or disable tens of millions of people

00:02:25.760 --> 00:02:27.660
wouldn't even exist anymore.

00:02:27.660 --> 00:02:30.790
And half the kids who are gonna die today
would not die.

00:02:30.790 --> 00:02:34.300
In short, the world's people would be healthier,
and better educated, and more productive.

00:02:34.300 --> 00:02:38.959
And that would be good for all of us, because
here's the thing: we do not actually live

00:02:38.959 --> 00:02:39.959
in two worlds.

00:02:39.959 --> 00:02:43.520
It sometimes feels like we do, not just because
no one in my neighborhood has kids who are

00:02:43.520 --> 00:02:47.960
stunted due to malnutrition, but because no
one I follow on Tumblr or Twitter or Instagram

00:02:47.960 --> 00:02:48.960
has kids who are.

00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:52.310
Hank, the reach of my online life can feel
infinite, but it isn't.

00:02:52.310 --> 00:02:54.710
And that inevitably creates an empathy gap.

00:02:54.710 --> 00:02:57.880
As Bill and Melinda put it in the letter,
there is overwhelming evidence that people

00:02:57.880 --> 00:03:01.849
care about others who are suffering when they
can see that suffering.

00:03:01.849 --> 00:03:05.720
But too often we can't see it, not because
people in the developing world are silent,

00:03:05.720 --> 00:03:08.040
but because we're not good at listening to
them.

00:03:08.040 --> 00:03:12.360
We're far better at listening to voices that
feel closer, that we can find on Tumblr or

00:03:12.360 --> 00:03:14.110
Twitter--people who feel like "us."

00:03:14.110 --> 00:03:19.010
But the way we imagine us must include people
living in poverty, because there is just the

00:03:19.010 --> 00:03:20.060
one world.

00:03:20.060 --> 00:03:24.080
And when we start to pay attention to problems
that seem distant and foreign, we often find

00:03:24.080 --> 00:03:25.440
that they actually aren't.

00:03:25.440 --> 00:03:27.660
They're human problems; they're us problems.

00:03:27.660 --> 00:03:31.000
Hank, I think the coolest thing about the
Gates letter this year is that they give us

00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:35.430
the opportunity to learn to listen better
and more broadly by signing up to be something

00:03:35.430 --> 00:03:37.099
they call a Global Citizen.

00:03:37.099 --> 00:03:40.730
The idea is that if we become more informed
about the issues facing people around the

00:03:40.730 --> 00:03:42.930
world, we will naturally become more engaged.

00:03:42.930 --> 00:03:46.250
I think that's brilliant, and I hope it allows
me, at least, to learn to listen better.

00:03:46.250 --> 00:03:48.739
So I hope you'll join me in signing up, link
in the doobly-doo.

00:03:48.739 --> 00:03:52.800
Hank, by the way, I also hope that we're still
making videos in 15 years so we can discuss

00:03:52.800 --> 00:03:55.780
which of the Gates letter predictions came
true and which didn't.

00:03:55.780 --> 00:03:59.180
But before then--in fact, significantly before
then--I will see you on Friday.

